# Every route the router declares, classified by whether it CREATES, REPLACES or DESTROYS a credential of the
# caller's own identity — the acts that must re-prove the current password and spend the one per-identity
# budget they all share (`CurrentPasswordProofThrottle`).
#
# Why this file exists: the third such route, `POST /me/recovery-secret/revoke`, first shipped WITHOUT the
# proof, on the argument that destroying a credential "grants nothing". A hostile read showed a stolen session
# could then remove the account's last recovery edge permanently and hold the account shut on the email-keyed
# lockout. No gate was looking at this axis, so the failure it guards against is a FOURTH route arriving —
# `POST /me/api-token/revoke`, say — with deptrac, PHPStan and every other registry green.
#
# The shared bucket is half the invariant, not an economy: no wrong `currentPassword` on any of these routes
# feeds the persisted lockout, so the limiter is the only ceiling on guessing that password from a live
# session, and a route with a bucket of its own hands the holder of a stolen session more guesses.
#
# Format, one per line. `::` is the field separator, so no field may contain it:
#   <route-name> :: credential-affecting
#   <route-name> :: ordinary
#   <route-name> :: anonymous :: <how the act is proven without a session, or why it is no credential act>
#
# `<route-name>` is the key of `api/.route-manifest.json`, the committed PROD router inventory that
# `make php.lint.route-manifest` holds equal to the router. The universe is that file, never a list kept here.
#
# What the gate (`make php.lint.credential-proof`: CredentialProofGateTest over the tree;
# CredentialProofRulesGateTest, CredentialProofActionRulesGateTest and PhpCallSitesTest for the falsifiability
# of its rules and of the reader that decides what a call is) checks:
#   - completeness and staleness against the route manifest, in both directions;
#   - an `anonymous` route's path matches a pattern in `api/.public-access-exemptions` (or its name a
#     `route:<name>` key there) — an authenticated route cannot opt out of the question by calling itself
#     anonymous;
#   - a `credential-affecting` route resolves (by reflecting `#[Route]`, so `self::ROUTE_NAME` and a
#     class-level name prefix resolve as Symfony resolves them) to exactly one controller ACTION whose own
#     body calls `ensureWithinBudget()` on the injected `CurrentPasswordProofThrottle` and `verify()` on the
#     injected `PasswordHasher`, and spends the budget BEFORE it invokes any method of an injected
#     `Application` use case whose own body calls `ProveCurrentPassword::ensure()`;
#   - the three acts that motivated the rule stay classified `credential-affecting`.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# What it deliberately does NOT do — a green proves what is below the line and nothing above it.
#
#   - A SESSION is not a credential here: it is proven by holding it and re-minted only by a credential.
#     `iam_revoke_other_sessions` / `iam_revoke_current_session` are `ordinary` on purpose — putting the
#     owner's eject path behind the shared budget would let a stolen session drain it and keep the owner from
#     evicting it (`RevokeOtherSessionsController`). A token that authenticates on its own (an API token) IS a
#     credential.
#   - It never judges a classification beyond the three pinned routes. `ordinary` over a genuinely
#     credential-affecting route passes; the completeness check forces the decision into a diff, and review is
#     the only control on that direction. `anonymous` is bounded only by the firewall registry.
#   - It checks that the collaborators are REACHED, textually and in controller order, not that the proof is
#     ordered correctly INSIDE the use case relative to the row lookup — the property that keeps the endpoint
#     from becoming an existence oracle. Nor that the call sits on every path: a branch that returns before
#     `ensureWithinBudget()` passes.
#   - Calls are matched as the token sequence `$this-><property>-><method>(` (the property name taken from
#     reflection) inside the bound action's body and the invoked use-case method's body — a comment or a
#     string literal is never a call, and a chain wrapped across lines still is one. A call through a local
#     alias, a helper method, a sibling method or a trait lies outside those bodies and reds (fails closed);
#     so does a proof reached through a second-level collaborator.
#   - It checks THAT the budget is spent and the password verified, not WHAT with: the key handed to
#     `ensureWithinBudget()` (it must be the identity id, or the bucket stops being per-identity) and the
#     arguments handed to `verify()` are unread.
#   - It says nothing about the throttle's own configuration (`rate_limiter.yaml`) — that every route spends
#     the SAME bucket is true only because the three share one injected service.
#   - The `anonymous` check matches the manifest's path TEMPLATE against the exemption regexes and has no HTTP
#     method dimension, as `.public-access-exemptions` itself has none.
#   - Routes outside the prod router (`/api/test/*`, `#[When(env: 'dev')]`) are not in the universe, because
#     the manifest is derived from the prod kernel.
#   - Acts over ANOTHER identity's credentials (an administrator unlocking or erasing a user) are `ordinary`
#     here by definition; they are governed by permissions, not by re-proving the caller's own password.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

